#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aba063f-68f3-54ea-8159-9de3326c4244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354981} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd6f81a-e323-57f7-b4c5-91be57463d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354982} {:answer/id #uuid "c3823b39-f9d1-59e4-b3e6-8a6a17d06a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354983} {:answer/id #uuid "0d711c1a-0e4e-5193-a390-12b390a3c8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354984} {:answer/id #uuid "9eea97d7-cc62-5fb4-abf2-4ca9858210bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3823b39-f9d1-59e4-b3e6-8a6a17d06a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354983} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d7b0bca-2eb1-5822-9229-910cf3f64e31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56774d2b-76f9-551f-b926-8c1400b9f917" :question/math-academy-id "q-79710" :question/problem "Let $v_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $v_2=\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$. Form $P=(v_1\\ v_2)$ using these columns in this order, with their displayed scales unchanged. For the differential equation $x'(t)=Ax(t)$, define the new coordinates by $u(t)=Dx(t)$ where $D=P^{-1}$. Find the exact matrix $D$." :question/worked-solution "Use the given eigenvectors as the columns of $P$ in the stated order and with their shown scales:\n\n$$P=(v_1\\ v_2)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-3\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\det P=1\\cdot2-(-3)(-1)=-1.$$\n\nThe requested coordinate map is $u=Dx$ with $D=P^{-1}$. The inverse formula gives\n\n$$D=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&-3\\\\-1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nBoth $DP$ and $PD$ are the identity matrix, so this is the exact inverse. If $Av_i=\\lambda_i v_i$, then $AP=P\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$ and\n\n$$u'=P^{-1}Ax=P^{-1}APu=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)u.$$\n\nThe opposite coordinate direction is $x=Pu$, whose matrix is $P$, not $D$. Fixing column order and eigenvector scales is necessary to specify this exact inverse. For example, $-D$ also yields a decoupled system if one instead uses the rescaled coordinates $\\widetilde u=-u$, but it is not $P^{-1}$ for the specified $P$. The original generic phrase “the decoupling matrix” did not state these conventions. The clarified prompt makes the exact inverse unambiguous; the source accepted this inverse as option c."}]}
